comments.body:
@HansOlsson I agree that it's a good idea to "clearly state that division by zero (etc) is an error in Modelica." 
But looking at the list of cases provided in this issue:
Nevertheless, shouldn't we provide (min=0+ i.e. small?) for the cases where a parameter<0  is meaningless and parameter=0 would cause a division by zero? A message "Parameter is not in range ..." befor translation is much nicer than "Division by zero" during initialization.

Here is a case worth considering.

```modelica
model DivisionByDivisionByZero
    input Real x;
    Real y;
equation
    y = 1 / (1 / x);
end DivisionByDivisionByZero;
```

If we decide that any division by zero is an error, then we have an error here if `x` is zero. To me this model seems fine, I don't see any problems evaluating `1 / (1 / 0)` to 0. 

If it is an error, is a tool allowed to do symbolic simplification to rewrite the equation to `y = x`? If so, this model may or may not be valid depending on which simplifications the tool does.

Language group: Agreement that division by zero etc, is an error - but a matter of describing the details.

Hans:
```
model D
  input Real x;
  Real a,b;
equation
   a=b/x;
end D;
 extends D(a=time);
```

If `a` is known we can compute `b` as `a*x`, what if `x` is zero? Some tools do this, is this used in practice? We need to test.

